A string literal has a type, and it is `char*`.

primary_expr_string emitted the address and left current_target alone -- the
same omission the integer literal had before the note above it was written. A
literal therefore inherited whatever type the previous expression happened to
leave, and indexing one used the DEFAULT element size, eight:

    "abc"[1]     mov_x14,8 / mul_x0,x14,x0 / ldr_x0,[x0]
    g[1]         add_x0,x1,x0             / ldrsb_x0,[x0]

for the same access through a char* variable. The index scaled by eight and
eight bytes were loaded instead of one.

WHAT IT COST. tcc walks every one of its path lists with

    for (p = in; c = *p, c != '\0' && c != PATHSEP[0]; ++p)

and PATHSEP is ":". PATHSEP[0] read eight bytes of the wrong place, so the
separator was never recognised and tcc_split_path returned the entire
colon-separated list as a SINGLE path:

    tcc: error: library 'c' not found
            tried: /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu:/usr/lib:/lib:...:/usr/local/lib/c

The crt search escaped it only because CRTPREFIX is one path with no separator
in it. Every list-valued path -- libraries, system includes -- was broken.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, because the method is the point. The log had said
"library 'c' not found" for several rounds and no more, and rounds were spent
reasoning about multiarch triplets and configure flags. tcc-debug/0004 made the
error print the candidate it actually opened; the very next run showed the
whole list concatenated with "/c" on the end, and there was nothing left to
deduce. Every time this spike made the program SAY something it was solved in
one round; every time it was reasoned about instead, it took four.

`char` was the one primitive built and then dropped -- every other type
anything asks about is held in a global -- so there was no type to name here
even had the omission been noticed. It is now kept as `character`.

MEASURED

    difftest      70 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, both architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    emission      "abc"[1] now identical to g[1] on a char* variable

KNOWN GAP, deliberately left: `*"abc"` -- dereferencing a literal rather than
indexing it -- is rejected outright as "is not a defined symbol". A separate
hole in primary_expr_variable's star handling; tcc does not use the form.

diff --git a/cc_core.c b/cc_core.c
index e20da61..e535e8e 100644
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -899,6 +899,33 @@ void primary_expr_string(void)
 	char* unique_id = add_string_to_string_list(s);
 
 	emit_load_named_immediate(REGISTER_ZERO, "", unique_id, "primary expr string");
+
+	/* A STRING LITERAL HAS A TYPE, AND IT IS `char*`.
+	 *
+	 * This left current_target alone, exactly as the integer literal did
+	 * before the note below was written, so a literal inherited whatever type
+	 * the previous expression happened to leave. Indexing one therefore used
+	 * the DEFAULT element size -- eight -- and emitted
+	 *
+	 *     "abc"[1]     mov_x14,8 / mul_x0,x14,x0 / ldr_x0,[x0]
+	 *     g[1]         add_x0,x1,x0             / ldrsb_x0,[x0]
+	 *
+	 * for the same access through a char* variable: the index scaled by eight
+	 * and eight bytes loaded instead of one.
+	 *
+	 * tcc walks its path lists with
+	 *
+	 *     for (p = in; c = *p, c != '\0' && c != PATHSEP[0]; ++p)
+	 *
+	 * where PATHSEP is ":". PATHSEP[0] read eight bytes of the wrong place, so
+	 * the separator was never recognised and tcc_split_path returned the whole
+	 * colon-separated list as ONE path:
+	 *
+	 *     tried: /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu:/usr/lib:/lib:...:/usr/local/lib/c
+	 *
+	 * The crt search survived only because CRTPREFIX is a single path with no
+	 * separator in it. */
+	current_target = character->indirect;
 }
 
 void primary_expr_char(void)
